      "title": "What makes a policy limited",
      "narration": "Elena’s coworker shows her a brochure: two hundred dollars a day while in the hospital, for forty dollars a month. Is that health insurance? No. It is a limited policy. Limited policies restrict either the perils they cover, such as accidents only, or the amounts they pay, such as a fixed daily sum unrelated to the bill. They are useful supplements and poor substitutes. Because buyers so often mistake them for comprehensive coverage, the policy must carry a prominent notice, typically on the first page, stating that it is a limited policy and describing what it does not cover.",
